diff --git a/docs/design/sm70_e4m3_dynamic_context.md b/docs/design/sm70_e4m3_dynamic_context.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000..af6e196efc
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/design/sm70_e4m3_dynamic_context.md
@@ -0,0 +1,76 @@
+# SM70 E4M3 attention capacity from the model window
+
+## Purpose
+
+Extend the existing grouped long-context and compact scalar-tail E4M3 decode
+routes to the configured model window. These providers already ship in the FA2
+extension; this change extends their usable context range and validates it.
+
+The existing long-route qualification is 262144 tokens. A block table describes
+the engine's allocated window even when the current request is short. Serving
+with a larger window, for example Yarn at `--max-model-len 1048576`, can exceed
+provider page-table or workspace checks during graph warmup. Existing grouped
+optimizations therefore cannot safely serve that configuration as qualified.
+
+## Capacity and native compatibility
+
+`sm70_grouped_long.py` resolves `model_config.max_model_len` and queries two
+independent native revisions: grouped dynamic context and scalar workspace.
+Revision 1 admits the served window within the native integer-addressing limits.
+Older binaries retain their known 256K capability. New Python code alone cannot
+expand an older native provider's contract.
+
+The scalar provider must cover the grouped graph's entire context bound.
+A mixed build with an insufficient scalar ABI declines the scalar-tail route.
+External manifests retain their declared capabilities. Missing model
+configuration retains the conservative built-in qualification.
+
+Page-table admission accepts at most `ceil(context / page_size)` columns.
+Only E4M3 calls with a qualified long-context graph descriptor and the dynamic
+native ABI can exceed the ordinary grouped table ceiling. FP16 and other codec
+qualifications retain their existing boundaries.
+
+## Workspace and native launch
+
+The scalar provider allocates `ceil(max_model_len / 1024)` partitions before
+profiling and graph capture. Its cache key includes the resolved capacity and
+device. Serial layers and captured target graphs reuse the same FP32 numerator,
+maximum and sum buffers.
+
+The native scalar entry validates shape, device/layout, integer bounds and
+workspace coverage of the page-rounded table. Partition indexing uses `grid.x`,
+removing the 65535-partition `grid.z` restriction. Reduction processes weights
+in fixed-size shared-memory tiles while preserving ascending FP32 accumulation
+order and stable softmax normalization.
+
+The grouped kernel retains its 80 splits and arithmetic. Its E4M3 table check
+uses the structural integer limit; Python admission enforces the negotiated
+provider capacity. The existing operator names and execution family are reused.
+
+## Graph behavior and scope
+
+The existing graph manager consumes the family owner's context contract.
+Capture fixes workspace and launch grids for the served window. Replay uses
+device lengths without allocating or copying lengths to the host. No context
+buckets, scheduler policy or model-specific runner logic are added. Existing
+route policy and external manifests retain their meanings. No new configuration
+or environment variable is required.
+
+## Validation
+
+- CPU: model-window resolution, independent native revisions, mixed builds,
+  page rounding, invalid bounds, workspace cache isolation and ordinary fallback.
+- GPU: FP64 references over the same quantized KV, q1 through q8, multiple heads
+  and request batches, page/partition boundaries, zero lengths and restored live
+  graph inputs. Numeric cases extend through 2M tokens.
+- A separate overprovided-workspace launch uses 65537 partitions and a short
+  live sequence to exercise grid/reduction coverage without a long KV cache.
+- Full engine: Qwen3.8-27B NVFP4, DFlash block 8, TP4 on four V100s, E4M3 KV,
+  a 1M served window and 1024 generated tokens per measured request.
+  Performance compares the ordinary path with the existing optimized routes
+  enabled under the expanded context contract. The reported context range is
+  32K through 512K; kernel coverage beyond it is validated separately.
+
+Production edits occupy two CUDA units and three existing Python family owners.
+The family README describes the boundary. The regression fixture updates source
+fingerprints while retaining the original legacy admission predicate bodies.
